---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import MergeVideos from '@/components/tools/media/MergeVideos.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="merge-videos">
  <MergeVideos client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Two ways to join videos, and why it matters which one runs</h2>
    <p>
      To merge videos looks simple but is not. There are two different mechanisms. A tool that uses
      one without telling you often gives merged files with audio drift or a missing second clip.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Concat demuxer (copy)</th>
            <th>Concat filter (re-encode)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Requirement</td>
            <td>Every clip has the same codec, resolution and audio format</td>
            <td>None. Any clips can be joined</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>What happens</td>
            <td>Compressed packets are joined and indexed again</td>
            <td>Everything is decoded, normalised and encoded once</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Speed</td>
            <td>Seconds, even for gigabytes</td>
            <td>Around real time for the whole running length</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Quality</td>
            <td>Identical to the sources</td>
            <td>One generation of H.264 loss</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      This page inspects each clip first, chooses the better method automatically, and tells you which
      one ran and why. A table lists each clip’s codec, resolution and length as FFmpeg reported them.
    </p>

    <h2>What makes clips "matching"</h2>
    <p>
      Clips from the same camera, phone or export preset almost always match, and those merges take
      almost no time. Mixed sources rarely match. The usual differences:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Resolution: a 4K phone clip and a 1080p screen recording.</li>
      <li>Codec: an H.264 MP4 and a VP9 WebM.</li>
      <li>Audio: AAC in one file, Opus in another, or one clip with no audio.</li>
      <li>Frame rate: 30 fps and 60 fps, or variable-frame-rate phone footage.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On the re-encode path, each clip is scaled to fit the largest frame size in the set and padded
      with black, not stretched, so nobody in the footage is distorted. All clips are normalised to
      30 fps with one AAC soundtrack.
    </p>

    <h2>The silent-clip trap</h2>
    <p>
      When one clip in a set has no audio track, naive concatenation gives a file where the sound runs
      ahead of the picture from that point on. This tool generates silence of exactly the correct
      length for each clip without sound, so the two streams stay the same length and all later clips
      stay in sync.
    </p>

    <h2>Ordering</h2>
    <p>
      The clips join from top to bottom in the list. Drag a row to move it, or use the arrow buttons on
      each row, which work with the keyboard. Files go into the list in the order you drop them, and
      files you drop later go at the end.
    </p>

    <h2>How many, and how big</h2>
    <p>
      The tool has no limit on the number of clips or their size. The limit is your device’s memory,
      because the inputs and the output are all in this tab. For very large sets, merge a few at a time
      and then merge the results. This uses less memory.
    </p>

    <h2>Before you merge</h2>
    <p>
      To trim clips first, <a href="/trim-video">trim video</a> cuts without re-encoding. If one clip
      has a different shape, crop it to match with <a href="/crop-video">crop video</a> first, so the
      merge can use the fast path. If the result must fit an upload limit, run it through
      <a href="/compress-video">compress video</a> afterwards.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
